# Contents list

A contents list provides a small, predictable map of a related guide.

These are civica.uk reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## Choose the navigation scope

This page owns contents-navigation semantics. Choose the scope before writing links:

| Need | Label and target | Related guidance |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sections of one article | “On this page”; stable heading fragments in reading order | [Publishing contents variant](../publishing/components/content-list.html) |
| Chapters of one short guide | “In this guide”; real page routes, with the current chapter identified | [Mini-hub composition](../patterns/mini-hub.html) |
| Independent tasks with saved progress | Task names and actual task status | [Complete multiple tasks](../patterns/complete-multiple-tasks.html) |

Do not mark every visible in-page fragment as the current page. For a multi-page guide, keep the contents order consistent and pair it with [previous and next links](pagination.html) naming their destinations. A retained publishing route is a source variant of this contract, not a second contents implementation.

## When to use it

Use it for a short group of related help pages, with matching previous and next navigation at the end. NHS guidance limits this mini-hub pattern to about eight pages.

## When not to use it

Do not use it as a substitute for the main application menu or for a multi-page form. A form needs task progression and saved answers, not a reference guide’s page list.

## Home Assistant adaptation

An Energy help guide can separate measurements, comparisons and missing data. Keep the page title and topic labels consistent with the contents. An in-page list is a local variant for one long article; use section fragments and label it “On this page” so the scope is clear.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Wrap the list in a labelled nav. Mark the current page when it really represents the current route. Use short descriptive links and keep the current topic distinguishable without colour.

